WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.700
Chapter 02 explains why programs
stall at Initial or low Advanced.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.775
They read CISA's maturity model
as a five-pillar shopping list.

3
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:13.128
But the model runs those pillars through
three cross-cutting capabilities,

4
00:00:13.128 --> 00:00:17.136
and the one programs fund least is
Governance, which the model defines

5
00:00:17.136 --> 00:00:21.213
as the definition and enforcement of
policy within and across pillars.

6
00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:24.358
Its Traditional stage reads
like the failure list:

7
00:00:24.358 --> 00:00:29.675
siloed pillars of policy enforcement, and
limited correlation of logs and telemetry.

8
00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:31.325
Five principles answer it.

9
00:00:31.325 --> 00:00:34.984
Identity is the anchor, but
it isn't self-authenticating,

10
00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:39.200
so sessions are re-evaluated and
the identity plane is defended.

11
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.973
Privileged identity gets
controls of its own:

12
00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:46.132
Microsoft's guidance is zero
permanently active assignments other

13
00:00:46.132 --> 00:00:50.000
than emergency accounts, which
are tested at least every 90 days.

14
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.213
Evidence must be reproducible,
machine-readable and regenerable.

15
00:00:54.213 --> 00:01:00.050
Governance enforces: a policy that only
produces next quarter's finding is a report.

16
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.045
And the cloud boundary is modeled,

17
00:01:02.045 --> 00:01:06.310
with each government-cloud feature
gap recorded as a governed finding.
